<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Kafka Pulse: Kafka vs Database</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; background: #f4f7f6; color: #333; padding: 20px; max-width: 1100px; margin: 0 auto; }
    h1, h2 { color: #2c3e50; }
    
    /* Concept Summary Block */
    .summary { background: #e8f4f8; border-left: 5px solid #3498db; padding: 15px 20px; border-radius: 5px; margin-bottom: 30px; }
    .summary ul { margin: 10px 0 0 0; padding-left: 20px; }
    .summary li { margin-bottom: 8px; line-height: 1.5; font-size: 15px; }

    /* Layout Grids */
    .grid-2 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 20px; margin-bottom: 30px; }
    
    /* Box Styles */
    .box { background: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; border: 2px solid #ccc; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); }
    .box h3 { margin-top: 0; text-align: center; background: #343a40; color: white; border-radius: 4px; padding-bottom: 10px; padding-top: 10px; font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 20px;}
    
    .highlight-text { font-weight: bold; color: #d35400; }

    /* --- ANIMATIONS SECTION --- */
    
    /* 1. Kafka Sequential Append Animation */
    .kafka-anim-container { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: flex-start; gap: 8px; background: #fdfefe; border: 2px dashed #3498db; padding: 15px; border-radius: 6px; height: 100px; position: relative; margin-bottom: 15px; }
    .tape-head { position: absolute; right: 15px; top: 15px; font-weight: bold; color: #2980b9; font-size: 12px; border-left: 3px solid #e74c3c; padding-left: 5px; height: 70px; display: flex; align-items: flex-end; }
    
    .log-block { 
      flex: 1; height: 60px; background: #3498db; color: white; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; font-weight: bold; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 13px;
    }

    /* CSS Keyframes for sequential appending */
    .blk-1 { animation: append1 4s infinite; }
    .blk-2 { animation: append2 4s infinite; }
    .blk-3 { animation: append3 4s infinite; }
    .blk-4 { animation: append4 4s infinite; }

    @keyframes append1 { 0% { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-10px); } 5%, 90% { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); } 100% { opacity: 0; } }
    @keyframes append2 { 0%, 25% { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-10px); } 30%, 90% { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); } 100% { opacity: 0; } }
    @keyframes append3 { 0%, 50% { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-10px); } 55%, 90% { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); } 100% { opacity: 0; } }
    @keyframes append4 { 0%, 75% { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-10px); } 80%, 90% { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); } 100% { opacity: 0; } }

    /* 2. Database Random Access & Locks Animation */
    .db-anim-container { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 8px; background: #fdfefe; border: 2px dashed #e74c3c; padding: 15px; border-radius: 6px; height: 100px; margin-bottom: 15px; }
    .db-cell { background: #ecf0f1; border-radius: 4px; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; font-size: 13px; font-weight: bold; color: #7f8c8d; transition: all 0.2s; border: 2px solid transparent; text-align: center; line-height: 1.2; }
    
    /* Random flashing to simulate locking and in-place updates */
    .lock-1 { animation: dbUpdate1 4s infinite 0.5s; }
    .lock-2 { animation: dbUpdate2 4s infinite 2.0s; }
    .lock-3 { animation: dbUpdate3 4s infinite 1.2s; }
    
    @keyframes dbUpdate1 {
      0%, 100% { background: #ecf0f1; color: #7f8c8d; border-color: transparent; }
      10% { background: #f1c40f; color: #333; }
      20%, 40% { background: #e74c3c; color: white; border-color: #c0392b; transform: scale(1.05); }
    }
    @keyframes dbUpdate2 {
      0%, 100% { background: #ecf0f1; color: #7f8c8d; border-color: transparent; }
      10% { background: #f1c40f; color: #333; }
      20%, 50% { background: #e74c3c; color: white; border-color: #c0392b; transform: scale(1.05); }
    }
    @keyframes dbUpdate3 {
      0%, 100% { background: #ecf0f1; color: #7f8c8d; border-color: transparent; }
      10% { background: #f1c40f; color: #333; }
      20%, 30% { background: #e74c3c; color: white; border-color: #c0392b; transform: scale(1.05); }
    }

    .rule-box { background: #d4edda; border-left: 5px solid #28a745; padding: 20px; border-radius: 5px; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; color: #155724; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); }
    .rule-box span.badge-db { background: #e74c3c; color: white; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 4px; font-family: monospace; }
    .rule-box span.badge-kafka { background: #3498db; color: white; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 4px; font-family: monospace; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Kafka vs Database: The Secret of Speed</h1>

  <div class="summary">
    <h2>Concept Summary: Why is Kafka so insanely fast?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Kafka's Secret:</strong> It is simply an <strong>Append-Only Log</strong>. It does not use complex data structures like B-Trees. It just writes raw data sequentially to the end of a file.</li>
      <li><strong>Hardware Sympathy (The Disk):</strong> Randomly jumping around a hard disk to find/update a record is slow. But writing <em>sequentially</em> to a hard disk is incredibly fast. Kafka exploits this physics.</li>
      <li><strong>No Locks, No Waiting:</strong> Because a Database allows in-place updates, it must <strong>Lock</strong> a row while updating it to prevent corruption. Locks cause traffic jams. Kafka <em>never</em> overwrites old data, so it requires zero locks.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <!-- KAFKA COLUMN -->
    <div class="box">
      <h3 style="background: #2980b9;">Kafka: Sequential Writes</h3>
      
      <!-- Kafka Animation -->
      <div class="kafka-anim-container">
        <div class="log-block blk-1">Offset 0</div>
        <div class="log-block blk-2">Offset 1</div>
        <div class="log-block blk-3">Offset 2</div>
        <div class="log-block blk-4">Offset 3</div>
        <div class="tape-head">Append<br>Only ➡️</div>
      </div>
      
      <p><strong>How it works:</strong> Data is continuously appended to the very end of the file. Older messages are never modified. Consumers simply scan forward.</p>
      
      <ul>
        <li><span class="highlight-text">Disk Access:</span> 100% Sequential.</li>
        <li><span class="highlight-text">Locking:</span> NONE. Readers and Writers never block each other.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- DATABASE COLUMN -->
    <div class="box">
      <h3 style="background: #c0392b;">Database: Random Access & Locks</h3>
      
      <!-- Database Animation -->
      <div class="db-anim-container">
        <div class="db-cell">ID: 1</div>
        <div class="db-cell lock-1">ID: 2<br>LOCKED</div>
        <div class="db-cell">ID: 3</div>
        <div class="db-cell lock-3">ID: 4<br>LOCKED</div>
        <div class="db-cell">ID: 5</div>
        <div class="db-cell">ID: 6</div>
        <div class="db-cell lock-2">ID: 7<br>LOCKED</div>
        <div class="db-cell">ID: 8</div>
      </div>

      <p><strong>How it works:</strong> Data is stored in complex trees. To update a record, the DB must randomly search the disk, lock the specific row, modify it, and rebalance.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><span class="highlight-text">Disk Access:</span> Random (Jumping around the disk).</li>
        <li><span class="highlight-text">Locking:</span> YES. Updates lock rows, causing contention.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- The Golden Rule Highlight -->
  <div class="rule-box">
    🔥 The Core Difference 🔥<br><br>
    Databases are optimized for <span class="badge-db">RANDOM ACCESS</span> and targeted updates.<br><br>
    Kafka is optimized strictly for <span class="badge-kafka">SEQUENTIAL ACCESS</span> and speed.
  </div>

</body>
</html>